---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-activecampaign';
const title = 'Privy vs ActiveCampaign for Shopify: Capture, SMS and CRM';
const desc = 'Compare Privy email, SMS and capture with ActiveCampaign Shopify sync and sales follow-up. Plan a wholesale inquiry handoff without duplicate messages.';

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Privy or ActiveCampaign better for a Shopify store doing $40k/mo?",
    "answer": "Revenue alone does not decide this. Compare the actual work: storefront acquisition and retail messaging, or an inquiry that needs a salesperson, a next action and a deal stage. Privy supports email and SMS automation as well as capture. ActiveCampaign adds a sales workflow option; validate its required plan and CRM add-on before choosing it."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can ActiveCampaign replace Privy for popup capture?",
    "answer": "Test the form experience you need rather than assuming an embedded form replaces a targeted popup. Privy offers storefront capture tools alongside messaging. If keeping Privy for capture, confirm the connection carries source and channel permission into ActiveCampaign; a captured address alone is not a working handoff."
  },
  {
    "question": "How does Privy vs ActiveCampaign pricing compare at 8,000 contacts?",
    "answer": "Use the same mailable audience, display traffic, SMS destinations and send schedule for both quotes. Privy separates email, SMS and display pricing dimensions. For ActiveCampaign, include the marketing tier, any CRM and SMS add-ons, seats and credits. An 8,000-contact count alone cannot establish a comparable total."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I use Privy and ActiveCampaign together?",
    "answer": "Possibly, when Privy capture and ActiveCampaign sales follow-up each have an assigned job. First prove the supported transfer path, consent fields, duplicate handling and unsubscribe behavior. Do not assume a native connector exists or turn off all Privy email before the replacement flows are tested."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Privy or ActiveCampaign?",
    "answer": "Include Sequenzy in a separate email evaluation if you want another lifecycle option. This comparison does not establish its current Shopify event coverage, SMS operation, pricing or CRM suitability. It should not be recommended as a wholesale pipeline replacement without that evidence."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which handles B2B wholesale better?",
    "answer": "ActiveCampaign is a candidate when wholesale inquiries need deal stages and assigned sales follow-up, subject to CRM entitlement. Privy can remain useful for retail capture and messaging. A wholesale tag is not a sales process: define sample requested, quote sent and next action, and keep retail promotions separate from quote correspondence."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which integrates with Shopify more natively for capture?",
    "answer": "Privy combines Shopify storefront capture with email and SMS marketing. ActiveCampaign documents order, product, customer and abandoned-cart data sync for Shopify. Capture placement and commerce data sync are different requirements; test both rather than ranking one integration as universally more native."
  },
  {
    "question": "What workflow test should decide the winner?",
    "answer": "Propose an inquiry-to-order rehearsal: capture separate email and SMS choices, route a sample request to its owner, place a Shopify order, stop any obsolete reminder, and test a reply and unsubscribe. Compare missing fields, duplicate sends and manual repairs. These are proposed acceptance tests, not results observed on either product."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Privy vs ActiveCampaign</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Privy vs ActiveCampaign for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">Choose around the customer journey you need to operate. Privy combines storefront capture with email and SMS marketing; ActiveCampaign connects Shopify commerce data to marketing and can extend into sales follow-up. A store selling retail products and handling wholesale sample requests may need both jobs, but it does not automatically need two senders.</p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-cyan-100/90">This is a workflow comparison, not a merchant trial. The examples below are proposed tests. No conversion lift, implementation time or recovered revenue has been measured here.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox picks={[
          { label: 'Capture and retail messaging', tool: 'Privy', reason: 'Evaluate its popups, email and SMS together against your storefront flows.' },
          { label: 'Inquiry ownership', tool: 'ActiveCampaign', reason: 'Evaluate a CRM-enabled plan when a person must own the quote and next action.' },
          { label: 'Split stack', tool: 'Prove the handoff', reason: 'Keep two tools only when permissions, source and suppression survive the connection.' },
        ]} />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Compare the work, then the subscription</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Privy evaluation</th><th>ActiveCampaign evaluation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Retail acquisition</td><td>Targeted displays and email/SMS signup experience</td><td>Forms and any retained storefront capture connection</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Retail follow-up</td><td>Email and SMS automation; not just a popup layer</td><td>Shopify data, automation entry and exclusion conditions</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Wholesale inquiry</td><td>Capture the request without confusing it with a retail offer</td><td>Deal owner, stage and next task with required CRM entitlement</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SMS replies</td><td>Demonstrate the handling path for your account</td><td>Check SMS add-on, dedicated number and inbox eligibility</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cost comparison</td><td>Mailable contacts, displays and SMS scope</td><td>Marketing plan, CRM/SMS additions and usage</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Start with an unfinished customer task</h2>
        <p>A retailer receiving sample requests from event planners has two distinct promises to fulfill: deliver the requested wholesale information, and send only the retail marketing the person selected. A discount popup and a quote request should not silently enter the same welcome offer. The decision is who owns each promise and how an order changes the next action.</p>
        <p>Privy is a reasonable candidate for a team that wants capture and retail messaging together. Its <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/privy">Shopify listing</a> includes email marketing, text messages and SMS automation. Calling it only a basic email or popup tool would miss part of the product. Test the actual welcome, cart and post-purchase branches you require before deciding another sender is necessary.</p>

        <h2>Where ActiveCampaign changes the operating model</h2>
        <p>A sample request can need an assigned salesperson, a quote stage and a reminder to call. ActiveCampaign offers CRM add-ons for sales workflows; include the needed entitlement rather than assuming pipelines are included in every marketing subscription. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/218788707-Automation-triggers-explained">automation trigger documentation</a> distinguishes marketing, CRM and SMS availability.</p>
        <p>For the proposed wholesale journey, make a sample request a sales action, while a newsletter signup remains a marketing preference. Define what should happen when the same person later buys retail: stop a redundant sales reminder, keep a genuinely open bulk quote, or ask the owner to review it. An order is evidence of a purchase, not proof that every inquiry is resolved.</p>

        <h2>Shopify sync is not the same as inquiry sync</h2>
        <p>ActiveCampaign documents <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/360012461899-Data-synced-from-the-ActiveCampaign-Shopify-integration">Shopify customer, order, product and abandoned-cart data</a>. Use that field inventory to design purchase-based exclusions. A wholesale form field or a quote status needs its own mapping; do not assume it arrives with an order.</p>
        <p>Before enabling historical import, read the <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/115000202150-Connect-your-Shopify-store-to-ActiveCampaign">connection and historical-sync instructions</a>. Reconcile a small sample of older customers against new guest and logged-in orders. Separate historical records from events that should start a new message. Keep live automations paused until you know which imported records can enter them.</p>

        <h2>A proposed sample-request rehearsal</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Create a retail signup, a wholesale request with no marketing selection, and a returning customer who does both. Record email and SMS permissions separately, along with source and timestamp.</li>
          <li>Submit the wholesale request twice. Inspect whether the chosen connection updates a person, creates duplicate contacts or opens two deals. Agree on one owner and one outstanding next action.</li>
          <li>Place a retail order using the same email. Inspect the synced order and the pending quote. Confirm only the intended purchase exclusions run; the quote must not disappear merely because a consumer order exists.</li>
          <li>Change the request email or phone number and inspect the matching behavior. Do not assume identity merges preserve every permission or sales note.</li>
          <li>Withdraw marketing permission, then inspect scheduled messages and both tools if used. Confirm the sales owner can see the preference before sending another promotion.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Retain event timestamps, field values, automation entries and the owner’s next action as the test record. Passing means each action matches your written specification. It does not mean either supplier has produced a revenue lift.</p>

        <h2>SMS needs a reply path as well as a send button</h2>
        <p>A buyer might reply to a sample reminder with a quantity or delivery-date question. Decide where that reply is worked and how the quote owner sees it. ActiveCampaign documents an <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/22691306940444-SMS-Inbox-Two-Way-Messaging">SMS inbox</a> for eligible Plus, Professional and Enterprise accounts with the SMS Marketing add-on and a registered dedicated number in the US, UK or Australia. Check your sending location and account eligibility before making that inbox part of the design.</p>
        <p>In a Privy demonstration, ask to send and answer that same sample question through the actual supported reply path; this comparison has not established its current reply scope. For either tool, test an ordinary reply, an opt-out and a message arriving when the salesperson is away. A Shopify SMS opt-in field does not by itself prove permission has transferred into a second sender with the required evidence.</p>

        <h2>Keep both tools only if the handoff earns its maintenance</h2>
        <p>A split can make sense when Privy owns an established retail capture program and ActiveCampaign owns sales follow-up. List exactly what crosses the boundary: contact identity, signup source, separate permissions, wholesale request, and suppression changes. The supported connector or middleware route remains an implementation prerequisite; no working native Privy-to-ActiveCampaign handoff is demonstrated here.</p>
        <p>Assign each automation to one sender before cutover. For example, retain the retail welcome in Privy while routing wholesale inquiries to sales, or move that welcome only after the replacement passes the same test. Disable a specific old flow after its pending entries are accounted for. Turning off all Privy email at once can interrupt flows that were never replaced.</p>

        <h2>Quote the same workload</h2>
        <p>Use <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/pricing">Privy’s pricing page</a> to distinguish mailable-contact email pricing, SMS and display scope. For ActiveCampaign, request the marketing tier needed for your Shopify setup plus the CRM entitlement, seats and SMS add-on you will actually use. Keep contract period, destinations, credit consumption and expected traffic visible in the quote. Neither an 8,000-contact list nor monthly store revenue supplies the missing inputs.</p>
        <p>Hypothetical internal-work example: suppose maintaining a split connection takes three hours monthly and reviewing duplicate inquiries takes two more. At an assumed $40 per hour, that is (3 + 2) × $40 = $200 per month of internal effort, before either subscription. Compare this assumption with measured staff time during a pilot; it is not a supplier price or a reason by itself to consolidate.</p>

        <h2>Where Sequenzy belongs in this decision</h2>
        <p>Sequenzy can be another candidate for a separate email evaluation. Its name does not solve the inquiry owner, consent transfer or SMS reply requirements described here. Before replacing either sender, establish its current Shopify events and suppression behavior against the same written flow. This page does not substantiate earlier pay-per-email or guided-playbook claims, or establish it as a CRM replacement.</p>

        <h2>Decision rule</h2>
        <p>Choose Privy when its capture and messaging cover the retail journey your team can operate. Evaluate ActiveCampaign when a defined sales process needs ownership and follow-up alongside Shopify data. Keep a split only after the handoff rehearsal succeeds and someone owns its failures. A revenue threshold or a label such as “advanced” cannot make that decision for the merchant.</p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-activecampaign">Drip vs ActiveCampaign</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-privy">Omnisend vs Privy</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-privy">Sequenzy vs Privy</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/b2b-wholesale">Email apps for B2B wholesale</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs ActiveCampaign FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
